 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; the server assigns each committed edit one event revision and stores it in the change store.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Committed revisions reach browsers over self-hosted WebSocket sessions. The delivery component owns those sessions: it accepts subscriptions, publishes each committed revision to every session watching that event, and holds the board state a reconnecting browser needs to resume. The change store keeps revisions replayable so a browser that reconnects after a gap can be brought up to the latest revision rather than left on stale state. Browser sync maintains the connection, reconnects when it drops, and catches up on the revisions it missed.
+
+Editors keep using the HTTPS command path; the WebSocket path carries committed changes outward only. Delivery latency is bounded by publish time rather than by a poll interval, so committed changes reach connected browsers well inside the 15-second target.
+
+Running the socket tier ourselves is accepted work: capacity planning for concurrent connections, and reconnect and catch-up behavior in both the server's session handling and the browser. We take that work in exchange for not depending on a hosted realtime provider.
 
